\begin{abstract}
% Mobile health (mHealth) systems help researchers monitor and care for patients in real-world settings. These technologies show promise in improving mental health treatment by utilizing Ecological Momentary Assessments (EMA), passive sensing data, and timely interventions. Many mHealth systems rely on emotion recognition algorithms, which require EMA responses for ground truth, making responsiveness crucial. While methods to increase receptivity exist, they often negatively affect data collection. Passive sensing tools and machine learning can help deliver EMAs when users are more responsive; however, certain emotions are associated with responsiveness, potentially causing an imbalance in reported emotions. We propose the Smart Trigger, a machine learning-based EMA delivery mechanism based on a multi-objective function that maximizes response likelihood and enhances affect prediction through uncertainty sampling. This method can improve responsiveness while collecting responses beneficial for affect prediction. We tested the multi-objective function offline from two datasets: 91 Alzheimer's Disease and Related Dementia (ADRD) spousal caregivers and 45 healthy participants. Our findings indicate that the Smart Trigger sends EMAs when participants are more receptive and experiencing less commonly reported emotions, improving receptivity and balancing reported emotions.
Mobile health (mHealth) systems help researchers monitor and care for patients in real-world settings. Many studies that utilize mHealth applications use Ecological Momentary Assessments (EMAs), passive sensing, and contextual features to develop emotion recognition models, which rely on EMA responses as ground truth. Due to this, it is crucial to consider EMA compliance when conducting a successful mHealth study. Utilizing machine learning is one approach that can solve this problem by sending EMAs based on the predicted likelihood of a response. However, literature suggests that this approach may lead to prompting participants more frequently during emotions associated with responsiveness, thereby narrowing the range of emotions collected.

We propose a multi-objective function that utilizes machine learning to identify optimal times for sending EMAs, thereby increasing the likelihood of response and capturing a broader range of emotions. The function identifies optimal moments by combining predicted response likelihood with model uncertainty in emotion predictions. Uncertainty would lead the function to prioritize time points when the model is less confident, which often corresponds to underrepresented emotions. We demonstrate that using this objective function to guide EMA delivery would result in prompts being sent when participants are responsive and experiencing less commonly observed emotions. Prioritizing less commonly observed emotions promotes emotional diversity, defined as the range of emotions a person experiences over time, leading to a more comprehensive picture of overall well-being.
% Furthermore, we evaluate how this approach may improve data collection compared to traditional random EMA scheduling. 

The evaluation is conducted offline using two datasets: (1)  91 spousal caregivers of individuals with Alzheimer’s Disease and Related Dementias (ADRD), (2) 45 healthy participants. Results show that the multi-objective function tends to be higher when participants respond to EMAs and report less commonly observed emotions. This suggests that using the proposed objective function to guide EMA delivery could improve receptivity rates and capture a broader range of emotions. 
% Finally, we show that the proposed objective function improves receptivity rates and the diversity of reported emotions compared to a traditional EMA delivery method.
\end{abstract}

