% Ecological Momentary Assessments (EMAs) enable the collection of individuals' emotional experiences in real time and are a core component of many mHealth studies. In our research, we aimed to improve this aspect of data collection, specifically by increasing the number of responses through improved EMA compliance. However, prompting participants only when they are likely to respond can introduce bias, as emotional state and responsiveness are often related. To overcome this, we developed a machine learning-based approach that determines the most effective times to send EMAs. Our method balances two important factors: identifying when a person is most likely to respond and detecting when their emotional state is less predictable. By prioritizing these moments, we aim to enhance participant responsiveness and capture a broader range of emotions. We evaluated our method with both caregivers of individuals with Alzheimer's and healthy adults. This research will help inform how we can digitally engage with participants in more thoughtful and effective ways, improving the interaction between users and mHealth technologies.

