\begin{abstract}

We study the densest subgraph problem and its variants through the lens of learning augmented algorithms. For this problem,  Charikar's greedy algorithm (APPROX 2000) provides a linear-time \( 1/2 \)-approximation, while computing the exact solution typically requires solving a linear program or performing maximum flow computations.

We show that given a partial solution---i.e., one produced by a machine learning classifier that captures at least a \( (1 - \epsilon) \)-fraction of nodes in the optimal subgraph---it is possible to design an extremely simple linear-time algorithm\footnote{These algorithms can be implemented in just a few lines of code.} that achieves a provable \( (1 - \epsilon) \)-approximation. Our approach also naturally extends to the directed densest subgraph problem and several NP-hard variants.

An experiment on the Twitch Ego Nets dataset shows that our learning-augmented algorithm outperforms both Charikar’s greedy algorithm and a baseline that directly returns the predicted densest subgraph without additional algorithmic processing.

\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\paragraph{Motivation and problem description.} Computing the densest subgraph is a well-studied problem in graph algorithms and data mining. Given a graph $G=(V,E)$, the density of a subgraph induced by a subset of nodes $H$ is defined as 
\[
    \ds(H)=|E(H)|/|H|.\] 

The densest subgraph problem asks to find the subgraph with the maximum possible density. Other interesting variants of this problem include the $k$-densest subgraph, at-most-$k$ densest subgraph, and at-least-$k$ densest subgraph problems \cite{Manurangsi17, BhaskaraCCFV10, KhullerS09, AndersenC09}.

The notion of density also extends to directed graphs. Given a directed graph $G=(V, E)$, the directed densest subgraph problem asks for two sets of nodes $S_1$ and $S_2$ that maximize 
\[
    \ds(S_1, S_2) = \frac{|E(S_1,S_2)|}{\sqrt{|S_1||S_2|}},
\]
where $E(S_1, S_2)$ is the set of edges  with source in $S_1$ and target in $S_2$. 

The densest subgraph problem, initially explored by Picard and Queyranne \cite{PQ82} and Goldberg \cite{Goldberg84}, has since become central to a wide range of applications, including community detection in social networks \cite{DGP07, CS12}, link spam detection \cite{GKT05, BXGPF13}, and computational biology \cite{FNBB06, SHKRZ10}. Over time, faster algorithms have been proposed for the problem and its variants \cite{GGT89, Charikar00, KS09}. Additionally, the problem has been extensively studied across various computational models, including streaming \cite{BHNT15, EsfandiariHW15, McGregorTVV15}, dynamic \cite{SJ20, BHNT15}, massively parallel \cite{BahmaniKV12, BahmaniGM14, GLM19}, and distributed settings \cite{SarmaLNT12, SuV20}. For an excellent survey, see \cite{LancianoMFB24}.

In this work, we study the densest subgraph and directed densest subgraph problems and some of their variants within the framework of learning-augmented algorithms. This approach is motivated by the observation that real-world data often exhibits underlying patterns that can be captured using machine learning. In this setting, we assume access to a predictor (e.g., a machine learning model) that provides a partial solution. Designing algorithms that incorporate machine learning predictions has seen a lot of recent success and become a popular paradigm. Examples of graph problems that have been studied in this paradigm are shortest paths, maximum matching, maximum independent set, min cut, and triangle counting \cite{FeijenGuido2021,BravermanDSW24, ChenSVZ22, MNS2025, Dong0V25, BoldrinV24}. Many other problems in algorithms and data structures in different computational models have also been studied in this setting. For a survey, see \cite{MitzenmacherV22}. A more comprehensive list of works can be found at \cite{algorithms_with_predictions}.

In the context of the densest subgraph problem, a predictor can be viewed as a classifier that predicts whether a node belongs to the densest subgraph, based on features such as its degree, average neighbor degree, and domain-specific attributes, if available.

\paragraph{The pitfall of blindly outputting the predictor's solution.} However, blindly relying on the predictor's output might result in a poor solution. The predictor may simply have low accuracy in predicting which nodes belong to the densest subgraph. This issue can often be overcome in practice by rigorous model testing.

More dangerously, even a reasonably accurate predictor can lead to poor approximations if not combined with additional algorithmic processing. One can devise a simple example in the context of the densest subgraph problem. Suppose we have a predictor that correctly identifies 90\% of the nodes in the densest subgraph. If the densest subgraph is a dense bipartite graph with bipartition $X,Y$ and $|Y| = 0.9 (|X| + |Y|)$ (i.e., $Y$ contains 90\% of the nodes of the densest subgraph), and the predictor provides us with $Y$, simply returning $Y$ results in a subgraph with density 0, which is arbitrarily worse than the optimal solution, even though the predictor has a 90\% ``accuracy''! See Figure \ref{fig:1} for an example.

