This definition may be interpreted as an approximation of the Weber's contrast~\cite{avat2023,otsu1975},
\[
W_c ~=~ \frac{I-I_b}{I_b},
\]
wherein $I$ represents the intensity of the object, while $I_b$ is the background intensity. On assuming $I_b <\!\!< I$, $W_c \sim {I}/{I_b}$. Now, if $I_b$ is specified by the rms of indicator values in the background region and $I$ represents the indicator value at every sampling point in the defect region, then the adopted contrast metrics may be described by ${\mathfrak{C}_{\text{\tiny mn}}} = \text{mean}({I}/{I_b})$ and ${\mathfrak{C}_{\text{\tiny mx}}} = \norms{\!{I}/{I_b}\!}_{\infty}$. This metric is intuitive and is inspired by the human visual system's relative sense of contrast~\cite{avat2023,otsu1975}. A higher value of the contrast metric means that the object is more distinguishable from its background. rms is a common statistical metric for quantifying signal fluctuations relative to background noise in imaging applications~\cite{zhang2018,szab2013}.





