To tackle the overhead raise by delta-copy, POS introduces \textbf{\textit{delta-restore}} over delta-copy, which shift the overhead from retransmitting dirty memories during checkpoint to recompute these memories based on DAG during restore. 
Specifically, one the one hand, with the constrution of the DAG, POS is aware of the creation and operation context of each active GPU buffers, so it's able to regenerate these dirty memories by reexecute corresponding kernels based on existing buffer checkpoints according to the instruction of DAG, bypassing the overhead of redumping these memories in delta-copy phase which would stall the process. We will discuss the details of delta-restore in~\ref{}. 
On the other hand, POS doesn't totally eliminate the delta-copy phase, as one should prevent the delta-restore of certain memory from re-executing all launched kernels since the process started, which would cause significant restore overhead. 
Instead, POS inserts delta-copy phase at a more sparse frequency compared to pre-copy phase, ensuring end-to-end performance won't be affected by delta-copy while limiting the overhead for regenerating missing checkpoint during restore.


