\subsection{Concurrent GPU buffer checkpointing}
\label{sec:checkpoint}
\noindent
{\sys} overlaps kernel execution with checkpointing. 
To fix the inconsistency caused by concurrent GPU execution, 
there are generally two strategies in existing literature: \\[-18pt]
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,leftmargin=10pt,itemindent=0pt]
    \item \textbf{Copy-on-write(CoW)-based.} 
    CoW pessimistically isolates concurrent updates to the same memory buffer, 
    thereby ensuring the buffer being checkpointed cannot become dirty. 
    On the CPU, we only need to mark the page table entries as CoW, 
    and the CPU execution will trigger the page fault so that the OS can do the isolation. 
    Unfortunately, the GPU has no such support.
     \\[-18pt]

    \item \textbf{Delta-copy-based. }
    Alternatively, we can optimistically overlap the checkpointing with the execution, 
    and re-transmit dirty pages (delta) once the checkpoint is done (delta-copy). 
    The delta can be detected by tuning the page table's dirty bits. 
    The retransmission can be done iteratively.     \\[-18pt]
\end{itemize}

\noindent
The first strategy is typically used in fault tolerance cases, 
as it does not require the latest checkpoints. 
On the other hand, the second strategy is used in situations like migration with pre-copy, 
since we need to resume the exact execution states upon the switch. 
We design the above strategies with the help of a kernel DAG ({\fig{fig:pos-copy}}):



\stitle{Soft dirty-bit. }
