\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

\noindent
We have implemented {\sys} from scratch in 2,4885  C++ LoC, 
excluding the GPU virtualization framework, communication libraries
and integration with CRIU. 
We have carefully tuned our virtualization frameworks following 
existing methods~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2401-13354,DBLP:conf/ipps/FinglerZYJWR22} 
such that virtualization has no overhead to our evaluated applications.

\stitle{Testbed. }
All our experiments are conducted on two local servers, 
each with eight A100 (80\,GB HBM, SXM4) GPUs, two Intel Xeon Gold 6348 CPUs (total 56 cores), 
and 1\,TB of host memory.
Two servers are connected via a 200\,Gbps network (ConnectX-6 InfiniBand).
Except for the live migration experiments that requires 2 GPUs (so we use two A100s),
other experiments only use a single A100. 
Both servers install CUDA 12.3~\cite{cuda123}. 

\stitle{Baselines. }
To the best of our knowledge, no available open-source OS-level C/R systems exist 
that can work on recent NVIDIA GPUs like A100.
Therefore, we've implemented the state-of-the-art system---Singularity~\cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2202-07848} in our codebase as the baseline (\textbf{Base}). 
Specifically, we've implemented the stop-the-world GPU C/R described in the original paper. 
We've carefully optimized its performance, 
e.g., leveraging pin memory to saturate the PCIe bandwidth during C/R.
We also apply our CPU-GPU coordinated checkpoint described in \textsection{\ref{sec:impl}} to it.
Meanwhile, we also compare {\sys} with Pytorch's default checkpoint method, 
a task-level checkpointing mechanism that supports fault tolerance (\textbf{Task}).
For task-level restore, we directly compare with starting the application's GPU process from scratch. 
For task-level migration, we omit its comparison because, to the best of our knowledge,
no existing task-level C/R system can lively migrate GPU applications like llama-2.

\stitle{Evaluated applications. }
We evaluated various ML tasks (see Table~\ref{tab:app-data}), 
ranging from training to inference, 
whose domains span vision (ResNET), language (BERT), 
large foundational models (llama-2 and GPT-2), 
and reinforcement learning (PPO).
The tasks are chosen from different applications 
that use various task libraries, aiming to show that {\sys}, 
generalizes to various applications.

