    From Figures \ref{fig:pointLoadPlotsA} and \ref{fig:pointLoadForms} it can be seen that when self-weight is low ($\rho g \leq 1.65$), the optimal structure employs the most direct load-path from the point load to the support, aligning the structure to the $\sqrt{2}L$ length span between diagonally opposing supports. However, when self-weight is more dominant (e.g. $\rho g \geq 1.85$) it is preferable to locate as much material as possible close to the shorter spans of length $L$ between adjacent supports, with a secondary set of elements spanning from the point load to these outer elements. For intermediate values of self-weight, the transition occurs by a splitting of the original, diagonal elements. 





