\citet{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2109-13986} claim that the seq2seq model learns how to copy patterns, however, does not generalize well in primitives that require dividing coefficients (e.g. $\int k_1 \cos(k_2) x dx = \frac{k_1}{k_2} \sin(k_2 x)$). Our model does not need to learn how to perform arithmetic operations, but rather has to recognize the pattern, copy a subexpression, and choose what action to apply. We believe that this is the main reason why we manage to obtain a more robust model than direct antiderivative prediction by \cite{deeplearningforsymbolicmath}. We also note that our method is more general in the sense that it can be applied to any step-by-step computation task with no way of verifying the correctness of the result. 


