The Semantic Search Process, as depicted in Figure \ref{fig:image_2}, is an intricate system designed to facilitate the retrieval of data based on semantic similarity rather than literal matching. This system is particularly adept at understanding the context and nuances of user queries, thereby providing more relevant and accurate search results.

The interaction model of the semantic search process can be elucidated through the following sequential steps:

\paragraph{User Query} The process commences with the user submitting a query to the system. This query is not merely a set of keywords but a comprehensive input that the system aims to understand in its entirety.

\paragraph{Embedding} Upon receiving the query, the system translates it into a semantic vector \cite{Moka-Massive-Mixed-Embedding}, an embedding that encapsulates the essence of the query. This transformation is critical as it allows the system to interpret the query in a manner that can be effectively used for comparison with the data stored.

\paragraph{Data Store} The Data Store plays a dual role in this process. Firstly, it serves as the repository where the vectorized data is stored, each uniquely tagged by a semantic vector. This tagging is a preprocessing step that enables efficient comparison with the vectorized query. Secondly, the Data Store integrates the user's input with the system's output, embedding them into a unified representation that is then stored in the database. This dual function allows the system not only to retrieve data but also to learn from each interaction, refining its understanding and improving future search accuracy.

\paragraph{Retrieval} The system then performs a comparison between the query vector and the vectors representing the data in the store. It calculates the semantic similarity to identify the top k most relevant data.

\paragraph{Output} The system presents the user with the top k most relevant data that closely matches the semantic vector of the query. This output is the culmination of the semantic search process, providing the user with data that is not only relevant but also contextually significant. \\

\noindent
This interaction model is a testament to the power of semantic search in understanding the underlying meaning of user queries and its ability to deliver data that is contextually rich and meaningful. It shifts the paradigm from keyword matching to a more sophisticated level of interaction that is akin to human understanding, bridging the gap between user intent and data retrieval.

