\subsection{Testing On Different Scale of Data}

The performance test was conducted by varying the scale of data stored in the Bhakti database, ranging from 1 to 5000 data entries. For each scale, the query time was measured in milliseconds (ms). The test was performed twice for each data scale: once without using an inverted index for pre-filtering (represented by the green line) and once with the inverted index enabled for pre-filtering (represented by the blue line). The purpose of this approach was to assess the impact of using an inverted index on query performance.

The results, as depicted in the provided Figure \ref{fig:image_4} show a clear trend in the query times for both scenarios. Initially, as the data scale increases from 1000 to 4000, the query times for both methods increase gradually. However, beyond 4000 data entries, the query times for both methods begin to rise more sharply.

A significant observation is the performance difference between the two methods. The green line, representing queries without the use of an inverted index, shows consistently higher query times compared to the blue line, which represents queries with an inverted index. This indicates that the use of an inverted index significantly improves the query performance of the Bhakti database.

The testing also reveals that at around 4000 data entries, the query times for both methods approach or exceed 1 second. This threshold is particularly noteworthy, as it suggests that the Bhakti database may not be the most suitable choice for scenarios involving very large datasets, where query times of 1 second or more could be considered impractical.

