Thereafter, we establish two supplementary results concerning key properties of the sentences $\varphi_{1}^{i}$ and $\varphi_{2}^{i}$, which will help us establish the main line of our argument.

\vspace{-0.7cm}

\begin{displayquote}
\begin{lemmaA}
\phantomsection
\label{lemma:A}
Let $\ i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$. \enspace If \enspace $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$, \enspace then\newline $[\varphi_{1}^{i}] \cap \Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{i}] \Big) = \varnothing$.
\end{lemmaA}
\vspace{-0.4cm}
\begin{proof}
Assume that $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$. By the specification of the sentence $\varphi_{1}^{i}$, we have that $[\varphi_{1}^{i}]=[K_{i+1}]$. Let $r\in[\varphi_{1}^{i}]$. Hence, $r\in[K_{i+1}]$ and $r\notin[K_{i}]$, meaning that $r\in$ {\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{i},K_{i+1}\big)$. Now, let $j<i$ and suppose towards contradiction that $r\in[K_j]$. Then, \mbox{$r\notin$ {\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{j},K_{i+1}\big)$}, which contradicts \mbox{{\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{j},K_{i+1}\big) \supseteq$ {\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{i},K_{i+1}\big)$}, imposed by condition (SD). Hence, \mbox{$r\notin[K_j]$}. Thus, we have shown that $[\varphi_{1}^{i}] \cap \Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{i}] \Big) = \varnothing$.
\end{proof}
\end{displayquote}

\vspace{-1cm}

\begin{displayquote}
\begin{lemmaB}
\phantomsection
\label{lemma:B}
Let $\ i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$. Then, $[\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}] \cap \Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{i}] \Big) = \varnothing$.
\end{lemmaB}
\vspace{-0.4cm}
\begin{proof}
By the specification of the sentence $\varphi_{2}^{i}$, we have that \mbox{$[\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}] \subseteq [K_{i+1}]\setminus[K_i]$} (either $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$ or $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] \neq \varnothing$). Let $r\in[\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}]$. Hence, \mbox{$r\in[K_{i+1}]$} and $r\notin[K_{i}]$, meaning that $r\in$ {\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{i},K_{i+1}\big)$. Now, let $j<i$ and suppose towards contradiction that $r\in[K_j]$. Then, \mbox{$r\notin$ {\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{j},K_{i+1}\big)$}, which contradicts \mbox{{\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{j},K_{i+1}\big) \supseteq$ {\em Dist}$_B\big(K_{i},K_{i+1}\big)$}, imposed by condition (SD). Hence, \mbox{$r\notin[K_j]$}. Thus, we have shown that \mbox{$[\varphi_{1}^{i}] \cap \Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{i}] \Big) = \varnothing$}.
\end{proof}
\end{displayquote}

% ---------------------------------------------------------- %

\noindent\underline{Belief-Change Functions:} Let $i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$, let $\ast$ be a lexicographic AGM revision function, and let $\dotminus$ be a moderate AGM contraction function. Suppose that $\ast$ assigns (via (R)) at:

\begin{itemize}
\item $K_1$ a faithful preorder $\preceq_{K_1}^{\ast}$ over $\mathbb{M}$, such that $\min(\mathbb{M},\preceq_{K_1}^{\ast}) = [K_1]$, and for all \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus[K_1]$}, $r \approx_{K_1}^{\ast} r'$.\footnote{Hence, the faithful preorder $\preceq_{K_1}^{\ast}$ attributes equal plausibility (modulo $K_1$) to all possible worlds outside of $[K_1]$.}

\item $K_{i+1}$ a faithful preorder $\preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\ast}$ over $\mathbb{M}$, such that $\preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\ast}\ =\ \preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\dotminus}$.
\end{itemize}

Moreover, suppose that $\dotminus$ assigns (via (C)) at $K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}$ a faithful preorder $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}$ over $\mathbb{M}$, such that $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}\ =\ \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\ast}$.

% ---------------------------------------------------------- %

The following two lemmas establish properties of the preorders $\preceq_{K_{i}}^{\ast}$, $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}$, and $\preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\ast}$. In particular, Lemma~\hyperref[lemma:C]{C} points out that all $\preceq_{K_{i}}^{\ast}$, $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}$, $\preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\ast}$ coincide on their sub-domain \mbox{$\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big) \times \mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big)$}, while Lemma~\hyperref[lemma:D]{D} shows that all worlds in \mbox{$\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_i]\Big)$} are equally plausible with respect to both $\preceq_{K_i}^{\ast}$ and $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}$.

\vspace{-0.7cm}

\begin{displayquote}
\begin{lemmaC}
\phantomsection
\label{lemma:C}
Let $\ i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$. For any worlds $r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big)$,\newline $r\preceq_{K_{i}}^{\ast}r'$ \enspace iff \enspace $r \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus} r'$ \enspace iff \enspace \mbox{$r\preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\ast}r'$}.
\end{lemmaC}

\begin{proof}
Since the transition from $\preceq_{K_{i}}^{\ast}$ to $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\ast}$ satisfies condition (R2), it follows that, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in[\neg\varphi_{1}^{i}]$}, $r \preceq_{K_{i}}^{\ast} r'$ iff $r \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\ast} r'$. Observe, however, that \mbox{$\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big) \subseteq [\neg\varphi_{1}^{i}]$} (either $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$ or $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] \neq \varnothing$), hence, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big)$}, $r \preceq_{K_{i}}^{\ast} r'$ iff $r \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\ast} r'$.

Likewise, since the transition from $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}$ to $\preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\dotminus}$ satisfies condition (C2), it follows that, for any $r,r'\in[\varphi_{2}^{i}]$, $r \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus} r'$ iff $r \preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\dotminus} r'$. Observe, however, that \mbox{$\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big) \subseteq [\varphi_{2}^{i}]$} (either $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$ or $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] \neq \varnothing$), hence, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big)$}, $r \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus} r'$ iff $r \preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\dotminus} r'$.

Combining the above, and given that $\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}\ =\ \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\ast}$ and $\preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\ast}\ =\ \preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\dotminus}$ (by the definition of $\ast$ and $\dotminus$), we have that, for any $r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_i]\cup[K_{i+1}]\Big)$,

\begin{center}
$r \preceq_{K_{i}}^{\ast} r'$ \quad iff \quad $r \preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus} r'$ \quad iff \quad $r \preceq_{K_{i+1}}^{\ast} r'$,
\end{center}

\noindent as desired.
\end{proof}
\end{displayquote}

% ---------------------------------------------------------- %

\vspace{-1.2cm}

\begin{displayquote}
\begin{lemmaD}
\phantomsection
\label{lemma:D}
Let $\ i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$. For any worlds $r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_i]\Big)$, $r\approx_{K_i}^{\ast}r'$ and $r \approx_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus} r'$.
\end{lemmaD}
\vspace{-0.3cm}
\begin{proof}
First, we prove by induction on $i$ that, for any $r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_i]\Big)$, $r\approx_{K_i}^{\ast}r'$.

\vspace{2mm}

\noindent\underline{\em Base Case:} The construction of $\preceq_{K_1}^{\ast}$ implies that, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus[K_1]$}, $r\approx_{K_1}^{\ast}r'$. Thus, we have proven the base case (for $i=1$).

\vspace{2mm}

\noindent\underline{\em Induction Hypothesis:} Assume that, for any $r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_k]\Big)$, $r\approx_{K_k}^{\ast}r'$.

\vspace{2mm}

\noindent\underline{\em Induction Step:} By the induction hypothesis, we have that, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_k]\Big)$}, $r\approx_{K_k}^{\ast}r'$. Furthermore, Lemma~\hyperref[lemma:C]{C} entails that, for any $r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_k]\cup[K_{k+1}]\Big)$, $r\preceq_{K_{k}}^{\ast}r'$ iff \mbox{$r\preceq_{K_{k+1}}^{\ast}r'$}. Observe, however, that \mbox{$\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{k+1}]\Big) \subseteq$} \mbox{$\bigg(\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_k]\Big)\bigg) \cap \bigg(\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_k]\cup[K_{k+1}]\Big)\bigg)$}. Combining the above, we have that, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{k+1}]\Big)$}, $r\approx_{K_k}^{\ast}r'$, and moreover, \mbox{$r\preceq_{K_{k}}^{\ast}r'$} iff \mbox{$r\preceq_{K_{k+1}}^{\ast}r'$}. This again entails that, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_{k+1}]\Big)$}, \mbox{$r\approx_{K_{k+1}}^{\ast}r'$}, as desired.

\vspace{2mm}

By a totally symmetric argument, we can also prove that, for any \mbox{$r,r'\in\mathbb{M}\setminus\Big([K_1]\cup\cdots\cup[K_i]\Big)$}, $r \approx_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus} r'$.
\end{proof}
\end{displayquote}

% ---------------------------------------------------------- %

In the remainder of the proof, we will show that, for any $i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$, \mbox{$K_{i+1} = \big(K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}\big)\dotminus\varphi_{2}^{i}$}. To that end, let $i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$ and consider the two cases depending on whether the successive belief sets $K_i$ and $K_{i+1}$ are mutually inconsistent or not, i.e., depending on whether $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$ or \mbox{$[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] \neq \varnothing$}, respectively. 

\begin{itemize}
\item Assume first that $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$. Recall that, in that case, $[\varphi_{1}^{i}] = [K_{i+1}]$ and $\varphi_{2}^{i} \equiv \top$ (as $[\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}] = \varnothing$). Since $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] = \varnothing$, it follows that \mbox{$[K_i]\cap[\varphi_{1}^{i}] = \varnothing$}. Furthermore, combining Lemmas~\hyperref[lemma:A]{A} and ~\hyperref[lemma:D]{D}, we derive that, for any worlds $r,r'\in[\varphi_{1}^{i}]$, \mbox{$r\approx_{K_i}^{\ast}r'$}. Therefore, condition (R) entails that $[K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}] = \min([\varphi_{1}^{i}],\preceq_{K_i}^{\ast}) = [\varphi_{1}^{i}] = [K_{i+1}]$; hence, \mbox{$K_{i+1} = K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}$}. Since $\varphi_{2}^{i} \equiv \top$, we deduce from postulates $(K\dotminus2)$ and $(K\dotminus5)$ that $\big(K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}\big)\dotminus\varphi_{2}^{i} = K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}$ (Remark~\ref{rem_expansion} of Section~\ref{section_agm}). Consequently, $K_{i+1} = \big(K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}\big)\dotminus\varphi_{2}^{i}$.\footnote{Hence, the contraction of $\varphi_{2}^{i}$ from theory $K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}$, via $\dotminus$, yields a trivial operation.} 

\item Thereafter, assume that $[K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}] \neq \varnothing$. Recall that, in that case, $[\varphi_{1}^{i}] = [K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}]$ and \mbox{$[\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}] = [K_{i+1}]\setminus[K_i]$}. Since $[\varphi_{1}^{i}]\subseteq[K_i]$, it follows that the sentence $\varphi_{1}^{i}$ is consistent with the belief set $K_i$. Hence, we deduce from postulates $(K\ast3)$ and $(K\ast4)$ that $K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i} = K_i+\varphi_{1}^{i} = Cn(\varphi_{1}^{i})$ (Remark~\ref{rem_tautology} of Section~\ref{section_agm}). This in turn entails that $[K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}] = [K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}]$. 

\noindent Next, combining Lemmas~\hyperref[lemma:B]{B} and~\hyperref[lemma:D]{D}, we derive that, for any worlds \mbox{$r,r'\in[\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}]$}, $r\approx_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}r'$. Therefore, we conclude from condition (C) that \mbox{$\big[\big(K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}\big)\dotminus\varphi_{2}^{i}\big] = [K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}] \cup \min\Big([\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}],\preceq_{K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}}^{\dotminus}\!\Big) = [K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}] \cup [\neg\varphi_{2}^{i}] =$} $\big([K_i]\cap[K_{i+1}]\big) \cup \big([K_{i+1}]\setminus[K_i]\big) = [K_{i+1}]$. Hence, $K_{i+1} = \big(K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}\big)\dotminus\varphi_{2}^{i}$.
\end{itemize}

Thus, in any case, $K_{i+1} = \big(K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}\big)\dotminus\varphi_{2}^{i}$, as desired.

% ---------------------------------------------------------- %

\vspace{3mm}

Consequently, and since $\ast$ and $\dotminus$ satisfy postulates (DP1)--(DP4) and (DPC1)--(DPC4), respectively (Remark~\ref{rem_subclass_dp} of Section~\ref{section_iteration}), we have shown that there exist a DP revision function $\ast$, a DP contraction function $\dotminus$, and two sentences $\varphi_{1}^{i},\varphi_{2}^{i}\in\mathbb{L}$, such that \mbox{$K_{i+1} = \big(K_i\ast\varphi_{1}^{i}\big)\dotminus\varphi_{2}^{i}$}, for any \mbox{$i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$}, as desired.
\end{proof}

Based on the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm_backprop_dp}, we comment on the interpretation of the epistemic inputs $\varphi_{1}^{i}$ and $\varphi_{2}^{i}$ that mediate the transition between successive belief sets during the training process of a binary ANN. These sentences are not arbitrary, but are systematically constructed to reflect the symbolic effect of each parameter update on the network's Boolean behaviour. Since the evolution from $K_i$ to $K_{i+1}$ is driven by the learning process ---which in turn is governed by the training labels--- it follows that $\varphi_{1}^{i}$ and $\varphi_{2}^{i}$ encode propositional information induced by those labels.\footnote{Very roughly speaking, $\varphi_{1}^{i}$ identifies the minimal content that must be revised into $K_i$ to initiate alignment with $K_{i+1}$, while $\varphi_{2}^{i}$ isolates the residual content that must be contracted to fully obtain $K_{i+1}$.} 

Furthermore, if $T$ denotes the belief set corresponding to the target Boolean function implicitly defined by the training labels, and condition (SD) is respected at the sequence $K_1, \ldots, K_n$, then this sequence follows an inclusion-decreasing trend in terms of type-B distance from $T$. Thus, $K_{i+1}$ is {\em epistemically closer} to $T$ than $K_i$, and the transition $(K_i \ast \varphi_{1}^{i}) \dotminus \varphi_{2}^{i}$ can be seen as a symbolic operation that draws the network's knowledge state closer to the label-induced target knowledge. In this light, $\varphi_{1}^{i}$ and $\varphi_{2}^{i}$ act as epistemic surrogates of the training signal at each iteration, representing label-driven logical corrections to the ANN's internal representation. This characterization invites further study into the precise syntactic and semantic relationship between the ANN's training data and the propositional forms of $\varphi_{1}^{i}, \varphi_{2}^{i}$, opening new avenues for a fine-grained symbolic analysis of learning dynamics in binary ANNs.

Example~\ref{ex_backprop_dp} offers a concrete illustration of how the training process of a binary ANN can be modelled using DP-compatible AGM-style operations --- namely, lexicographic revision and moderate contraction. This is achieved by instantiating the construction mechanism outlined in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm_backprop_dp}.

