\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}



Standard KG-RAG preprocessing merges every mention of entities extracted from chunked document (e.g., \emph{Hermione Granger},
\emph{Professor Quirrell}, or the \emph{Sorcerer's Stone}) into a single
node—obliterating the time-specific information. Such nuance is needed to answer questions such 
as Query~\ref{q:hermione}. We therefore keep \textbf{each} mention---a concrete, context-specific instance of an entity as it appears in a single chunk of text (e.g., "Hermione" in Chapter 9 versus "Miss Granger" in Chapter 16) --- distinct and tether it to the \emph{event
snippet} in which it occurs. Intuitively, the structure is as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:entity-event-demo}. The left column holds \textit{entity mentions} and their contextual descriptions; the right column holds \textit{events} and their descriptions.
Treating the entity as two disjoint vertex sets ensures temporal facets never collapse.

\paragraph{Formal definition.}
Let  
\(
  \mathcal{G}_{\text{ent}}
     =(V_{\text{ent}},E_{\text{ent}})
\)
be the directed graph of entity mentions and  
\(
  \mathcal{G}_{\text{evt}}
     =(V_{\text{evt}},E_{\text{evt}})
\)
the graph of events.  
We connect the two with a bipartite edge set
% \begin{equation}
% \mathcal{B} \;=\;
% \bigl\{(v_i,e_j)\;\big|\;
%       v_i\in V_{\text{ent}},~
%       e_j\in V_{\text{evt}},~
%       \mathrm{Name}(v_i)\subset\mathrm{Desc}(e_j)\bigr\},
% \label{eq:bipartite}
% \end{equation}

\begin{align}
\mathcal{B}
  = \bigl\{(v_i,e_j)\,\big|{}&
      \;v_i \in V_{\text{ent}},\;
      e_j \in V_{\text{evt}},\notag\\[2pt]
  & \mathrm{Name}(v_i)\subset\mathrm{Desc}(e_j)
     \bigr\}.                       \label{eq:bipartite}
\end{align}

so that an edge exists if and only if the entity's name $\mathrm{Name}(v_i)$ appears in the event
description $\mathrm{Desc}(e_j)$.

% The triple
% \(
%   (\mathcal{G}_{\text{ent}},\mathcal{G}_{\text{evt}},\mathcal{B})
% \)
% is the backbone of our retrieval pipeline and will re-appear in Alg.~\ref{alg:retrieval}. 


\paragraph{Preprocessing.} 
% The preprocessing mirrors Algs.~\ref{alg:insertion} and~\ref{alg:bipartite}, and it unfolds in four steps:
\ours consists of the following preprocessing steps to build knowledge graphs\footnote{Pseudocode can be found as Algs.~\ref{alg:insertion} and~\ref{alg:bipartite} in the appendix.}.

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent \textit{1. Chunking.} 
      The document is split into chunks
      \(\{c_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{L}\) and stored in a key–value store.

% \item \textbf{Creating entity and event sets.}  
%       Each chunk \(c_\ell\) is fed twice to an LLM—
%       once with an \emph{entity} prompt and once with an \emph{event} prompt.
\vspace{0.5em}
      \noindent \textit{2. Creating entity and event sets.}  
       Each chunk \(c_\ell\) is fed twice to an LLM to extract \emph{entities} $V_{\text{ent}}^{(\ell)}$ and \emph{events} $V_{\text{evt}}^{(\ell)}$ respectively.
       % —once with an \emph{entity}
       % prompt and once with an \emph{event} prompt to undergo extraction. 
       For every entity and event extracted, we also ask the LLM for a one-sentence description. Note that in entity's case, this description will be \emph{context-aware} so the same entity extracted from different document chunks will have different descriptions\footnote{e.g.\ "Hermione—rule-obsessed first-year" versus "Hermione—pragmatic rule-breaker".}.
       This description is stored in the node's \texttt{description} field and is kept
       \emph{distinct} for every mention. The extractions form the node sets  
      \(V_{\text{ent}}=\!\bigcup_\ell V_{\text{ent}}^{(\ell)}\) and  
      \(V_{\text{evt}}=\!\bigcup_\ell V_{\text{evt}}^{(\ell)}\).
      Co-mentions inside the same chunk yield the directed edge sets
      \(E_{\text{ent}}\) and \(E_{\text{evt}}\) exactly as in LightRAG.

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent \textit{3. Link entities to events (\(\mathcal{B}\)).}  
      For every entity \(v\!\in\!V_{\text{ent}}^{(\ell)}\) we scan the events
      \(e\!\in\!V_{\text{evt}}^{(\ell)}\);  
      if the node's name \(\mathrm{Name}(v)\) appears in
      \(\mathrm{Desc}(e)\) we add the bipartite edge \((v,e)\) to
      \(\mathcal{B}\) (Eq.~\ref{eq:bipartite}).  

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent \textit{4. Embed and index.}  
      Each node's name and
      description is embedded with embedding model \(f(\cdot)\) and written to two
      vector stores—one for entities, one for events—while the full graph
      \(\mathcal{G}_{\text{ent}}\cup\mathcal{G}_{\text{evt}}\cup\mathcal{B}\)
      is saved for hop-limited traversals.



Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} shows the overall pipeline of \ours and Figure~\ref{fig:e2-KG} shows a subgraph of the Entity-Event KG of \textit{Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone} and an event node's meta data.



\paragraph{Query-time retrieval.}
\label{sec:retrieval}
Given a query $q$, the retrieval mechanism conducts the following steps.
% (or a HyDE variant; see \ref{sec:hyde-coupling}),
% we perform:

\vspace{0.5em}
\noindent \textit{1. Cue extraction.}  
% The entity and event LLM extractor maps \(q\) to phrase sets \(S_{\text{ent}},S_{\text{evt}}\).
A pair of entity and event phrase sets are first extracted by an LLM extractor $g(\cdot)$, namely $(S_{\text{ent}},S_{\text{evt}}) = g(q)$

\vspace{0.5em}
\noindent \textit{2. Embedding generation.} 
The $(S_{\text{ent}},S_{\text{evt}})$ will be fed into the embedding model $f(\cdot)$ and generate a embedding $z$ used for retrieval, namely $z = f(g(q))$.

\vspace{0.5em}
\noindent \textit{3. Seed nodes retrieval.} 
With the embedding $z$, the next step is to retrieve seed nodes vector stores \(V_q\subseteq V_{\text{ent}}\cup V_{\text{evt}}\).


\vspace{0.5em}
\noindent \textit{4. One-hop expansion.}  
We take one step over the bipartite edges,
\(V_q^{+}=V_q\cup\{e\mid\exists v\in V_q:(v,e)\in\mathcal{B}\}
             \cup\{v\mid\exists e\in V_q:(v,e)\in\mathcal{B}\}\),
guaranteeing that every retrieved entity comes packaged with its
time-specific events and vice-versa.

\vspace{0.5em}
\noindent \textit{5. Similarity ranking.}
All passages are embedded offline; at query time we select only the vectors
of nodes in $V_q^{+}$—typically top-$k$ based on their similarity instead of the full index. 
When multiple entity nodes correspond to the \emph{same}
entity, their similarity scores are re-ranked according to the similarity
ranking of their \emph{associated event} nodes.  This grounds each entity in the
events’ evidence, preserving temporal–causal consistency.


\vspace{0.5em}
\noindent \textit{6. Context assembly.}  
We collect  
(i) the raw passages behind the top-\(k\) nodes;  
(ii) a linearised dump of the subgraph
\(\mathcal{G}_{\text{sub}}
    =(V_q^{+},
      (E_{\text{ent}}\!\cup\!E_{\text{evt}}\!\cup\!\mathcal{B})
      \cap (V_q^{+}\!\times\!V_q^{+}))\); 
and  
(iii) the metadata (descriptions, labels, etc.) associated
with every selected node and edge. This enriched, structured context is fed to the backbone LLM, allowing it to answer questions that demand fine-grained temporal and causal reasoning
using the truly relevant document chunks.

Together, these two routines turn the intuition from Figure~\ref{fig:entity-event-demo} into a fully operational retrieval pipeline, with \(\mathcal{B}\) acting as the
critical "glue’’ that preserves evolving entity states across time.




% In this section, we describe our proposed \emph{\ours} creation. We begin by using the standard LightRAG pipeline to construct an entity KG: the document is first chunked by token count, each fragment is sent to the LLM to extract entities and their relationships, and those extractions are added into the graph. We then apply the exact same process to extract events (and their contextual descriptions) instead of entities, yielding a second event KG. Next, we build a bipartite graph that links each entity node to related event nodes: we index events by the same chunk identifiers and connect an entity to an event whenever the entity name appears in the event's name or description. These bipartite edges capture the involvement of entities in events. At inference time, the LLM pulls both entity and event keywords from the user's query (and any provided context), retrieves the top-$k$ most relevant entity and event nodes from their respective vector stores, prunes entities to those directly connected to the extracted events, and gathers the corresponding edges and source text passages. Finally, these selected nodes, relationships, and passages are formatted into a structured context string, which the LLM uses to generate a grounded answer that seamlessly integrates both entity and event knowledge.

