\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:Experiments}
% In the experiment, we use different modes of LightRAG and GraphRAG, HyDE, RQ-RAG and vanilla RAG as baselines. To evaluate the performance of each method, for each query, we give an evaluator LLM the query, the ground truth and the responses of the variant of \ours and all baselines. The evaluator LLM is asked to assign a score from 1 to 10, 10 being the highest quality and 1 being the lowest. To make sure the evaluation is consistent, we use two models as the evaluator: Claude-3.7-sonnet and GPT-4.1-mini and compare the results.
% \footnote{The reason we did not chose the more common pair wise rating method is that we observed the transitivity does not hold for the model ranking: even when ground truth is give, we observe it is quite common that the evaluator judges response A is better than B, B is better than C, but C is better than A. Furthermore, this type of result is observed for different evaluator LLMs (Claude-3.7 sonnet, GPT-4o, GPT4.1-nano, GPT-4.1-mini). This should not have happened because the ground truth is provided. Therefore, we conclude that this result is not reliable.}.

\paragraph{Baselines.} We compare the five variants of \ours (four hypothetical response variants, one without hypothetical response) against the three modes of LightRAG~\citep{guo2024lightrag} (local, global, hybrid), three modes of GraphRAG~\citep{edge2024local} (local, global, drift), RQ-RAG~\citep{chan2024rq}, vanilla HyDE~\citep{gao2023precise} as well as vanilla RAG. In addition, we also give the hybrid mode of LightRAG the same four variants with the hypothetical response (thirteen in total excluding all the variants of our own methods). All methods use GPT-4o-mini \citep{hurst2024gpt} as the backbone LLM for preprocessing, inference, or both, and text-embedding-3-small \citep{text-embedding-3-small} as the embedding model \(f(\cdot)\).
% \paragraph{Evaluation.}  For our experiments ( \ref{sec:Experiments}) we grade model answers with GPT-4o, GPT-4.1-mini and Claude-3.7-Sonnet on a 1–10 Likert scale, following the rubric in Appendix~\ref{subsec:ResponsesEvaluationPrompt}.  Each sample is scored independently by all three models; we report the ranking based on the mean score across all three LLM judges in Table~\ref{tab:overall-avg-scores}. The individual judge's results can be found in Appendix~\ref{appendix:AdditionalResults}.

% \begin{equation}
% \textstyle
% \operatorname{Score}=
% \frac1J\sum_{j=1}^{J}\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^{N}s_{ij},
% \label{eq:eval}
% \end{equation}



\paragraph{Evaluation.}
For each query, we give an LLM judge the query, the ground truth and the responses of the variants of \ours and all baselines. Each answer is graded independently by \textit{J}~=~3 LLM judges
(Claude-3.7-Sonnet, GPT-4o, GPT-4.1-mini \citep{openai_gpt4.1_2025}) on a 1–10 Likert scale using the
rubric in Appendix \ref{subsec:ResponsesEvaluationPrompt}.  The overall
quality score for a system is the mean of those ratings across all
samples and judges, computed as

\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Score} \;=\;
\frac{1}{J}\sum_{j=1}^{J} \;
\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} s_{ij},
\label{eq:eval}
\end{equation}

\noindent where $N$ is the number of question–answer pairs in the test set; $J$ is the number of LLM judges; and $s_{ij}\in\{1,\dots,10\}$ is the score assigned by judge $j$ to sample $i$.

We report the (\textit{mean score}) in Table \ref{tab:overall-avg-scores};
per-judge scores are provided in Appendix \ref{appendix:AdditionalResults}.

\paragraph{Result.} 
In the overall ranking, the top-3 modes are all hypothetical response variants of \ours, while \ours (vanilla) loses to GraphRAG local, GraphRAG drift and LightRAG hybrid mode. This corroborates that hypothetical responses play a major role in improving retrieval quality. In particular, the superior performance of the combined extraction variant, followed by the combined embedding variant, confirms that both incorporating the original query and employing the extraction step further enhance the retrieval effectiveness. In contrast, all hypothetical response variants of LightRAG hybrid mode performed poorly. We speculate that because \ours does not employ de-duplication, it synergizes particularly well with the hypothetical response, achieving top performance regardless of the specific variant used. Furthermore, the hypothetical response provides a richer context and an abundant number of entity and event candidates. This can be beneficial to the KG matching when there are event nodes to provide grounding to the entities to mitigate hallucination. Figure \ref{fig:heatmap} plots the category-average scores (averaged over the three evaluator LLMs).  In every category, the top-scoring model is again an \ours hypothetical response variant. For more details, refer to Appendix~\ref{appendix:AdditionalResults}.


