NAS-BENCH-201 is a widely used cell-based search space comprising 15,625 distinct neural architectures.
Each cell in NAS-Bench-201 includes 6 layers and 5 predefined operations for each layer. 
Within the NAS-BENCH-201 search spacee, we perform conditional generation of architectures across four task datasets and subsequently train the top-performing architecture to validate their performance, as shown in \Cref{nb201_experiments}. 
In experiments on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, EDNAG achieves comparable performance on test accuracies compared to previous methods. 
In Aircraft and Pets, EDNAG surpasses previous baselines with improvements of \textbf{2.23}\% and \textbf{10.45}\% on test accuracies, respectively. 
Across diverse task datasets, EDNAG outperforms all previous NAS and NAG methods, achieving state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance and showcasing exceptional capability in generating optimal architectures. 
This is attributed to the FD strategy, which efficiently guides architecture samples toward high-fitness subspaces during the iterative generation process, serving as a powerful conditional mechanism to steer architectures toward global optimality.
Compared to DiffusionNAG, the previous SOTA baseline that directly generates neural architectures using naive diffusion models with limited optimization abilities, EDNAG excels with its superior global optimization capabilities while maintaining efficient generative abilities. 

Moreover, EDNAG also significantly improves the efficiency of architecture validation by reducing the number of architectures (Archs) requiring training to achieve final accuracy. 
For CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, the final accuracies are obtained from the NAS-Bench-201 benchmarks~\cite{dong2020bench} by querying only the \textbf{top-1} generated architecture. 
For Aircraft and Pets, only the \textbf{top-1} architecture requires full training in EDNAG, enhancing the validation efficiency of \textbf{3}$\times$ and \textbf{2}$\times$ compared to DiffusionNAG. 
The results illustrate that the top-1 neural architecture identified by our neural predictor attains optimal accuracy after training and testing, demonstrating its remarkable performance, achieved by the application of coreset selection before dataset encoding. 
Unlike the random sampling method used in DiffusionNAG, this approach significantly enhances the stability and reliability of the predicted accuracies. 

