\subsection{Ablation Study}
\label{ablation_study}

\begin{figure}[htb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{FD.pdf}
    \caption{Comparison of architecture generation using naive diffusion models, diffusion models with FD strategy, and diffusion models with both FD and optimization strategies.}
    \label{FD_abalation}
\end{figure}

The Fitness-guided Denoising (FD) strategy introduces evolutionary algorithms to emulate the iterative denoising process in diffusion models, which constitutes the core mechanism of EDNAG. 
Furthermore, the optimization strategy, driven by selection operators, further enhances the ability of EDNAG to generate globally optimal neural architectures. 
To deeply analyze their critical roles, we conducted a comparative analysis of the architecture generation process under three distinct conditions: naive diffusion models, diffusion models with the FD strategy, and diffusion models with the FD strategy and optimization strategy, as illustrated in \Cref{FD_abalation}.

Naive diffusion models often result in convergence to local optima, resulting in suboptimal architecture generation. 
The integration of the FD strategy significantly enhances the accurancy of generated architectures, facilitating the generation of globally optimal solutions. 
In addition, the incorporation of the optimization strategy substantially accelerates the convergence of the denoising process. 
These results underscore the effectiveness of both the FD strategy and the optimization strategy within EDNAG, demonstrating their pivotal roles in achieving task-optimal architecture generation. 

