As the pioneering network-free NAG approach, EDNAG achieves minimal computational resource consumption and rapid conditional generation. 
To demonstrate its efficiency, we compare EDNAG with DiffusionNAG in terms of computational costs during both training and inference processes in the NAS-Bench-201 search space, as shown in \Cref{search_time_EDNAG_DiffusionNAG}. 
Since EDNAG is free of training, it takes \textbf{0} GPU hours for the training process, while DiffusionNAG requires 3.43 GPU hours in the NAS-BENCH-201 search space on a Tesla V100-SXM2. 
For generation process, we evaluate the inference time of both EDNAG and DiffusionNAG on an NVIDIA RTX 4090. 
EDNAG achieves speedups of up to \textbf{100}$\times$, \textbf{100}$\times$, \textbf{7}$\times$, and \textbf{5}$\times$ compared to DiffusionNAG on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, Aircraft and Pets, respectively. 
Therefore, EDNAG not only enables neural architecture generation without time-consuming training processes but also significantly reduces computational cost during the inference process.
This is attributed to the FD strategy during the generation process, which directs the denoising process without relying on a score network, thereby achieving remarkable execution efficiency. 
In contrast, DiffusionNAG depends on a complex score network at each step of the generative process to predict the noise to be removed, which demands extensive and frequent network inference, significantly slowing down the generation process.

