\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed P2P energy markets with uniform pricing using the concept of DOEs to address the social acceptance issues arising from locational pricing. By introducing a new degree of freedom for trading DOE limits, we showed that the uniform pricing approach does not compromise grid capacity and is equivalent to the traditional locational pricing approach. Our design is based on competitive markets where participants are price takers. We demonstrated that the resulting market equilibrium, known as the competitive equilibrium, maximizes social welfare, and vice versa. We further investigated the relationship between the proposed uniform pricing markets and the traditional locational pricing market. We showed that, in the locational pricing approach, a budget surplus is generated, whereas, in the uniform pricing approach, the market is strongly budget-balanced, meaning the total payments from participants sum to zero. Finally, we evaluated our methods through a case study of EV charging on an IEEE 13-node test feeder and discussed the effectiveness of the proposed approaches. Future work could explore extensions to systems with uncertainties, where model predictive control potentially addresses challenges related to uncertainties associated with renewable energy generation, demand, and the timing of controllable loads such as EVs.
