   The proof of the reverse direction is based on strong duality, which follows from the satisfaction of Slater's condition. Recall that $\mathbb{U}_i =\{ \mathbf U_i| \underline{\mathbf u}_i\leq \mathbf u_i(t) \leq \overline{\mathbf u}_i;  \underline{\mathbf x}_i \leq \mathbf x_i(t) \leq \overline{\mathbf x}_i, \text{for } t \in \mathcal{T}\}$ for  $i \in \mathcal{N}$,  which is a polyhedral set. For any $(\mathbf U, \mathbf P, \mathbf L)$ such that $\mathbf U_i \in \mathbb{U}_i$, the Lagrangian function of \eqref{eq2} is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq15}
